WEBVTT

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:22.270
<v Ira Glass>This Christmas, Luke was in Iraq with a sniper unit in the Marines on a little forward operating base in Ramadi, maybe 200 guys-- bunk beds, dirt floors, pretty basic. They'd go out for two days at a time, set up a covert position in a house or a building, with a long range rifle, monitor what was going on in the street, shoot at guys who were setting up bombs in the road.
<v Ira Glass>这个圣诞节，卢克和海军陆战队的一个狙击小组待在伊拉克拉马迪的一个小型前沿作战基地里，大约200人——上下铺，泥土地面，条件相当简陋。他们每次出任务两天，在一个房子或建筑物里架设隐蔽点，用远程步枪监视街上的动静，射击那些在路上安放炸弹的人。

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:35.770
<v Luke Huisenga>The people we encountered, mostly I don't think they were really well-trained. And if we're doing our job right, they don't necessarily know where they're getting shot at from. So that makes it harder for them to turn around and return fire.
<v Luke Huisenga>我们遇到的人，大部分我觉得他们并没有受过很好的训练。如果我们工作做得对，他们甚至不知道自己是从哪里被击中的。所以这让他们很难转身还击。

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:43.690
<v Ira Glass>Spending hour after hour on watch, his is like the military cliche-- lots of extended boredom, broken up by moments of great intensity.
<v Ira Glass>在值班时消磨掉一小时又一小时，他的经历就像军事老套路——大量漫长的无聊，被偶尔极其紧张的时刻所打断。

00:00:43.690 --> 00:01:02.550
<v Luke Huisenga>And then you're constantly trying to then stay sharp and pay-- you know. It might be boring. There might not be anything going on, but you sort of have to stay-- try and keep focused and stay vigilant and sort of think your way into it not being boring and having your brain turned on.
<v Luke Huisenga>然后你得一直努力保持敏锐，注意——你知道的。也许很无聊。可能什么事都没有，但你必须保持——试着保持专注，保持警惕，并试着通过思考来让自己不觉得无聊，让大脑保持运转。

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:17.120
<v Ira Glass>Men, these tough guys, Marine snipers, will come off duty, go back to their base. There wasn't much entertainment, not the greatest internet back then. This is 2005. So they watched DVDs, specifically this.
<v Ira Glass>男人们，这些硬汉，海军陆战队的狙击手们，下哨回到基地。那里没什么娱乐活动，那时的网速也不怎么样。那是2005年。所以他们看DVD，特别是这个。

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.920
<v Lorelai>Well, I believe three minutes is plenty of time for some coffee.
<v Lorelai>好吧，我相信三分钟冲杯咖啡足够了。

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.270
<v Rory>Yes, coffee, please!
<v Rory>是的，咖啡，谢谢！

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.350
<v Lorelai>Hey, we're dying. Load us up.
<v Lorelai>嘿，我们要死了。给我们来点。

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:24.630
<v Luke>It isn't ready yet.
<v Luke>还没准备好。

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:24.980
<v Lorelai>What?
<v Lorelai>什么？

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:25.970
<v Rory>Mommy.
<v Rory>妈咪。

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.690
<v Lorelai>What do you mean it's not ready? It's 6:00 in the morning. Nothing says coffee like 6:00 in the morning.
<v Lorelai>你说没准备好是什么意思？现在是早上6点。没有什么比早上6点喝咖啡更合适的了。

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.360
<v Ira Glass>And what is this?
<v Ira Glass>这是什么？

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:37.440
<v Luke Huisenga>That's the Gilmore Girls.
<v Luke Huisenga>那是《吉尔莫女孩》。

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:55.300
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, the Gilmore Girls, the story of single mom Lorelai Gilmore, her teenage daughter Rory, her mom Emily, and the small town they live in, fictional Stars Hollow, Connecticut, beloved by a generation of tween girls and their mothers-- and other people too.
<v Ira Glass>是的，《吉尔莫女孩》，讲述单身母亲罗蕾莱·吉尔莫、她十几岁的女儿罗里、她的母亲艾米丽，以及她们居住的小镇——虚构的康涅狄格州星谷，被一代青春期女孩和她们的母亲所钟爱——当然还有其他人。

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.160
<v Ira Glass>It's a very girly show.
<v Ira Glass>这是一部非常女孩气的剧。

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.350
<v Luke Huisenga>It's a girly show. The girl is right there in the title, sure.
<v Luke Huisenga>这是一部女孩气的剧。标题里就有“女孩”，当然。

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.280
<v Luke Huisenga>[LAUGHTER]
<v Luke Huisenga>[笑声]

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:12.140
<v Ira Glass>I mean, there are a lot of scenes of like, you know, a mom and her daughter talking about how they're obsessed with coffee.
<v Ira Glass>我是说，剧里有很多场景，你知道的，妈妈和女儿谈论她们对咖啡有多痴迷。

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:39.410
<v Luke Huisenga>Yeah. I mean, I'm of two minds on how girly it is. I mean, I think that's part of what we liked about it too. Because there were zero women around where we were, and also because I think my platoon, my friends, we sort of liked doing things that were different and weirding people out a little bit.
<v Luke Huisenga>是啊。我是说，对于它有多“女孩气”，我有两种想法。我是说，我觉得这也是我们喜欢它的原因之一。因为我们周围根本没有女性，而且我觉得我的排、我的朋友们，我们有点喜欢做些与众不同的事，让别人觉得有点怪怪的。

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.820
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:02:41.820 --> 00:03:16.900
<v Luke Huisenga>You know, and it would sort of bother the crusty old first sergeant who was always yelling at us about things and making our lives miserable. It would sort of bother him if we were watching a girly show or if you wore a pink t-shirt to a company event, things like that. But he couldn't yell at you like you were doing something incorrectly like he could everything else. So it was almost like a little bit of a rebellion that the sniper platoon was all holed up watching Gilmore Girls.
<v Luke Huisenga>你知道，这会让那个总是对我们大吼大叫、让我们生活痛苦的古板老军士长感到困扰。如果我们在看一部女孩气的剧，或者在公司活动时穿粉色T恤之类的，他会很困扰。但他又不能像指责其他事情那样指责我们做得不对。所以，狙击排全窝在一起看《吉尔莫女孩》，这简直像是一种小小的反叛。

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:29.000
<v Luke Huisenga>Here's a typical scene. It's about being a mom and being a daughter. Lorelai, who's the single mom on the show, is being criticized by her mother about the way that she's raising her daughter, Rory.
<v Luke Huisenga>这是一个典型片段。关于做母亲和做女儿。剧中单亲妈妈罗蕾莱，正被她母亲批评她抚养女儿罗里的方式。

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.450
<v Emily>Lorelai Gilmore, I've watched you do a lot of stupid things in your life, and I have held my tongue.
<v Emily>罗蕾莱·吉尔莫，我看着你这辈子做了不少蠢事，我一直忍着没说。

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:34.770
<v Lorelai>Ha, you've what?
<v Lorelai>哈，你做了什么？

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:38.400
<v Emily>But I will not stand by and let you allow that girl to ruin her life.
<v Emily>但我绝不会袖手旁观，让你任由那女孩毁掉自己的人生。

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.520
<v Lorelai>Mom, back off.
<v Lorelai>妈，离远点。

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.240
<v Emily>She spent the night out with that boy, the one you let her run off to that dance with.
<v Emily>她和那个男孩在外过夜，那个你让她私奔去跳舞的男孩。

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.980
<v Lorelai>Mom, so help me god, I will not get into this with you.
<v Lorelai>妈，看在上帝的份上，我不会跟你争这个。

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:49.230
<v Emily>She's doing the same thing you did.
<v Emily>她在走你的老路。

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:49.860
<v Lorelai>No, she's not.
<v Lorelai>不，她没有。

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:50.640
<v Emily>She's going to get pregnant.
<v Emily>她会怀孕的。

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:51.830
<v Lorelai>No, she's not.
<v Lorelai>不，她不会。

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.320
<v Emily>She's going to ruin everything just like you ruined everything.
<v Emily>她会把一切都毁了，就像你毁了一切一样。

00:03:54.320 --> 00:04:00.360
<v Lorelai>No, she's not! No, she's not! No, she's not! Rory is a good kid, Mom. She's not me.
<v Lorelai>不，她不会！不，她不会！不，她不会！罗里是个好孩子，妈。她不是我。

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:00.570
<v Emily>What kind of mother are you?
<v Emily>你是个什么样的母亲？

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:03.180
<v Ira Glass>So yeah, Marine, what do you see?
<v Ira Glass>所以，海军陆战队员，你看到了什么？

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:26.160
<v Luke Huisenga>Yeah, I mean, it is girly. And there's a lot of women talking. And I know there's the whole scale. Like, two women can't talk on screen, like men won't watch the show. There has to be a man or they're talking about a man. Yeah, it's girly, but it's also a funny show. It's a smart show. It's a well-written show.
<v Luke Huisenga>是啊，我的意思是，它确实很女孩气。而且里面有很多女人在说话。我知道有个不成文的规矩：如果有两个女人在屏幕上说话，男人们就不会看这部剧。除非有个男人，或者她们在谈论一个男人。没错，它是很女孩气，但它也是一部有趣的剧。它很聪明。剧本写得很好。

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:45.060
<v Luke Huisenga>I think first, it's good. And I don't think we would have watched it if it wasn't good, the same reason everyone else watched it. But I think it struck a particular chord with us as sort of escapism, a different world from where we were.
<v Luke Huisenga>我觉得首先，它很好看。我不认为如果它不好看我们会去看，原因和其他人看的原因一样。但我认为它作为一种逃避现实的方式，某种远离我们所在世界的不同世界，与我们产生了特别的共鸣。

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:52.260
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, yeah. It's hard to imagine like a world further away from a war zone in Ramadi.
<v Ira Glass>是啊，是啊。很难想象一个比拉马迪战区更遥远的世界。

00:04:52.260 --> 00:05:13.420
<v Luke Huisenga>Right. Yeah. It's a really idyllic kind of world, but it's not super sappy. It still has sarcasm and weirdness that makes the idyllic part of it-- the really nice warm part of it-- it sort of makes it even more palatable and real maybe.
<v Luke Huisenga>没错。是啊。那是一个非常田园诗般的世界，但它又不是超级多愁善感。它依然有那种讽刺和怪诞，让田园部分——那非常美好温暖的部分——变得更容易让人接受，甚至更真实。

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:35.850
<v Ira Glass>Luke says they all learned about the Gilmore Girls from their corpsman Jess, whose sister watched the show and got hooked himself after he noticed a Tom Waits reference in one episode. He's into Tom Waits. A few of the guys started watching. Other guys who complained that it was on, and then they reluctantly get pulled in themselves until they had a core group-- Jess, Erik, John, Greg, seven or eight guys in all-- who watched everything.
<v Ira Glass>卢克说他们都是从卫生兵杰斯那里了解到《吉尔莫女孩》的，杰斯的妹妹看这部剧，而杰斯在注意到其中一集提到汤姆·威茨后自己也入坑了。他喜欢汤姆·威茨。几个家伙开始看。其他抱怨的人后来也勉强加入了，直到他们形成了一个核心小组——杰斯、埃里克、约翰、格雷格，总共七八个人——他们看了每一集。

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:55.940
<v Ira Glass>Hey, where were you and your friends on the show's big controversies? Like Rory had three guys over the course of the different seasons. There was Jess. There was Logan. And there was Dean. And for people who haven't seen the show, it's sort of like Jess was the rebel. Logan was the rich kid. And Dean was the townie, maybe a little boring.
<v Ira Glass>嘿，对于剧里的大争议，你和你的朋友们站在哪一边？比如罗里在不同季节里有三个男人。有杰斯。有洛根。还有迪恩。对于没看过这部剧的人来说，杰斯是叛逆者。洛根是富家子弟。迪恩是小镇男孩，可能有点无聊。

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:56.690
<v Luke Huisenga>Right.
<v Luke Huisenga>没错。

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:59.320
<v Ira Glass>Who did you guys side with?
<v Ira Glass>你们站在哪一边？

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:10.980
<v Luke Huisenga>So Erik was a big Dean supporter. There was a lot of-- like sort of a running gag of Erik coming to Dean's defense and us making fun of him for it.
<v Luke Huisenga>埃里克是迪恩的忠实支持者。当时有个梗，就是埃里克总是为迪恩辩护，我们以此取笑他。

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:14.510
<v Ira Glass>Well, Dean was married. I mean, Dean was married.
<v Ira Glass>可是，迪恩结婚了。我的意思是，迪恩已经结婚了。

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:19.390
<v Luke Huisenga>I think Erik would say that they were always true love, and the marriage doesn't count.
<v Luke Huisenga>我想埃里克会说那是真爱，婚姻不算数。

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:23.570
<v Ira Glass>Mhm. OK. And you, who were you with?
<v Ira Glass>嗯。好的。那你呢，你支持谁？

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:27.380
<v Luke Huisenga>I'm anti-Dean, and I'm kind of OK with Jess and Logan.
<v Luke Huisenga>我反迪恩，我对杰斯和洛根还算支持。

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:33.140
<v Ira Glass>I'm with you. I never had a problem with Logan. I think Logan was really-- he was so into her.
<v Ira Glass>我跟你一样。我从没觉得洛根有什么问题。我觉得洛根真的很——他真的很爱她。

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:36.090
<v Luke Huisenga>Yeah, he was good to her. And I think he was really-- she sort of needed--
<v Luke Huisenga>是啊，他对她很好。而且我觉得他真的——她确实需要——

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:51.770
<v Ira Glass>OK, we don't need to play any more of this part of the interview on the radio. You get the idea. They were real, actual fans. And probably this is a good time to out myself, if you haven't picked this up already. I have seen all the old episodes myself, though not the new recent Gilmore Girls reboot reunion shows.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我们不需要在广播里播放采访的这一部分了。你们明白意思了。他们是真实的、真正的粉丝。如果你们还没看出来，现在大概是我自曝身份的好时机。我自己也看过所有旧剧集，不过最近重启的《吉尔莫女孩》重聚节目没看过。

00:06:51.770 --> 00:07:03.340
<v Ira Glass>I have lots of opinions about the show, I should say, that Luke did not share. For instance, there's a whole host of cartoonishly wacky B plot townspeople characters I was not into.
<v Ira Glass>我必须说，我对这部剧有很多看法，卢克并没有分享这些看法。例如，剧里有一大堆漫画式的古怪配角，我就不太喜欢。

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:04.550
<v Luke Huisenga>You hate Kirk?
<v Luke Huisenga>你讨厌柯克？

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:06.150
<v Ira Glass>I hated Kirk.
<v Ira Glass>我讨厌柯克。

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:07.220
<v Luke Huisenga>Oh, Ira.
<v Luke Huisenga>哦，艾拉。

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:15.100
<v Ira Glass>I also believe the Gilmore Girls has the worst music cues of any series in the last three decades of television. Case in point.
<v Ira Glass>我还认为《吉尔莫女孩》是过去三十年里所有电视剧中音乐配乐最差的一部。举个例子。

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:19.560
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - SAM PHILIPS, "LA LA"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 山姆·菲利普斯，《啦啦》]

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:37.850
<v Luke Huisenga>I think the cues that you're talking about, like the la la la stuff, I mean, I think we sang that, you know, walking around. Well, like, (SINGING) La la la la la la la.
<v Luke Huisenga>我觉得你说的那些配乐，就是那些“啦啦啦”的东西，我是说，我想我们走路的时候也会唱。就像，（唱歌）啦啦啦啦啦啦啦。

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:54.700
<v Ira Glass>But that's not what we're here to talk about. I'm telling you this story because, of course, it's Christmas, and this is a Christmas story. Back in 2005, when they were in Ramadi, as Christmas approached, they were far from home, missing family and life there. Luke decided to go online.
<v Ira Glass>但那不是我们今天要谈的。我讲这个故事是因为，当然，现在是圣诞节，这是一个圣诞故事。回到2005年，当他们在拉马迪时，圣诞节临近，他们远离家乡，想念家人和那里的生活。卢克决定上网看看。

00:07:54.700 --> 00:08:12.910
<v Luke Huisenga>I was going to try and buy-- and I thought it would be a fun and funny gift to try and buy guys Gilmore Girls t-shirts or something as a Christmas present. And the Gilmore Girls website store at the time, all the clothing was either like a spaghetti strap tank top or a baby doll tee.
<v Luke Huisenga>我想买——我觉得给伙伴们买《吉尔莫女孩》的T恤或者之类的作为圣诞礼物会既好玩又有趣。但当时《吉尔莫女孩》的网站商店里，所有衣服要么是细肩带背心，要么是娃娃衫。

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:14.410
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:20.000
<v Luke Huisenga>There was not any unisex or male anything on there.
<v Luke Huisenga>上面没有任何中性或男性的东西。

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:24.020
<v Ira Glass>And you guys were bad asses. But you weren't going to go that far.
<v Ira Glass>而你们是硬汉。但你们不会走那么远。

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:26.150
<v Luke Huisenga>Yeah, well, I mean, it's not flattering.
<v Luke Huisenga>是啊，我是说，那并不讨好。

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:28.930
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. [LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>是啊。[笑声]

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:35.040
<v Ira Glass>And so he wrote a letter to the creator of Gilmore Girls, Amy Sherman-Palladino. Here's a bit of it.
<v Ira Glass>所以他给《吉尔莫女孩》的创作者艾米·谢尔曼-帕拉迪诺写了一封信。这里有一部分。

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:57.400
<v Luke Huisenga>"I have to tell you, my guys and I absolutely love your show. I know you must hear that all the time. I even read a recent endorsement of the show in Esquire magazine. Still, I'd venture to say that we are even further outside the Gilmore Girls target audience. I live in a strict, macho sometimes violent world that very often does not make sense. Stars Hollow, the small town where the Gilmore Girls takes place--"
<v Luke Huisenga>“我必须告诉你，我和我的伙伴们绝对喜欢你的剧。我知道你肯定经常听到这些。我甚至在《时尚先生》杂志上看到过对该剧的最近一次推荐。不过，我敢说我们甚至比《吉尔莫女孩》的目标受众还要遥远。我生活在一个严格、大男子主义、有时暴力的世界里，这个世界经常让人无法理解。星谷，那个《吉尔莫女孩》发生的小镇——”

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:02.920
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait, you explained to Amy Sherman-Palladino what Stars Hollow was in your letter? [LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等，你在信里给艾米·谢尔曼-帕拉迪诺解释星谷是什么？[笑声]

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:17.330
<v Luke Huisenga>Being reunited with the reality of this letter 10 years later has really disabused me of a lot of my illusions. Because I remembered the really good parts, and I thought I must of wrote a great letter. And then reading it again, I see like, oh man. This was not all good.
<v Luke Huisenga>10年后重温这封信的现实，真的打碎了我许多幻想。因为我记得那些特别好的部分，以为自己写了一封很棒的信。然后再读一遍，我发现，天哪。这并不全是好的。

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:28.010
<v Ira Glass>The rest of the letter actually is pretty good, a really nice summary of what he and his guys like so much about the TV show. And then at the bottom, there's a PS. "PS," he writes--
<v Ira Glass>信的其余部分其实很不错，很好地总结了他和他的伙伴们为什么那么喜欢这部剧。在底部，有一个附言。“附言，”他写道——

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:35.940
<v Luke Huisenga>"I checked the website, and the shirts appear to be cut for girls. What gives? I was going to buy the guys Christmas presents."
<v Luke Huisenga>“我看了网站，衬衫似乎是为女孩剪裁的。怎么回事？我本来打算买给伙伴们当圣诞礼物的。”

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:47.430
<v Ira Glass>Which, I have to say, was handled perfectly, just enough of a hint. A few weeks later, sure enough, packages arrive-- five big boxes.
<v Ira Glass>不得不说，这件事处理得很完美，暗示得恰到好处。几周后，果然，包裹送到了——五个大箱子。

00:09:47.430 --> 00:10:05.910
<v Luke Huisenga>And we opened them up, and they were full of these like navy blue wool jackets that had very subtle teal embroidering on the breast that said Gilmore Girls in the typeface and a really nice letter from Amy Sherman-Palladino.
<v Luke Huisenga>我们打开一看，里面装满了那种深蓝色的羊毛夹克，胸前有非常精致的青色刺绣，用那种字体写着《吉尔莫女孩》，还有艾米·谢尔曼-帕拉迪诺写的一封非常友好的信。

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:11.400
<v Ira Glass>These were crew jackets, made as gifts for the show's camera people and gaffers in men's sizes.
<v Ira Glass>这些是工作夹克，是作为礼物为剧组的摄像师和电工制作的男款尺码。

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:32.550
<v Luke Huisenga>It was a big morale boost for everybody. I remember taking them back and giving them out and sort of reading the letter. And even guys that didn't watch the show were excited.
<v Luke Huisenga>这对每个人的士气都是极大的鼓舞。我记得把它们带回去分发，还读了那封信。即使是没看过剧的伙伴们也很兴奋。

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:48.870
<v Luke Huisenga>Obviously the show meant a lot to my friends and I. I think I tried to tell her that she let us feel like it was ours a little bit.
<v Luke Huisenga>显然这部剧对我和我的朋友们意义重大。我想我试图告诉她，她让我们觉得这部剧也稍微属于我们了。

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:50.420
<v Ira Glass>Are you choking up?
<v Ira Glass>你哽咽了吗？

00:10:50.420 --> 00:11:01.550
<v Luke Huisenga>Yeah. Yeah. I just think it was really generous of her.
<v Luke Huisenga>是啊。是啊。我觉得她真的非常慷慨。

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:23.240
<v Ira Glass>It really felt like Christmas morning, he told me. The jackets were just what he wanted, even though he didn't even know that jackets like this existed. I wonder if it felt that way to Amy Sherman-Palladino also. You know? The letter Luke wrote to her, like a present she didn't even know she wanted, didn't even know she could want till it arrived. And then she was really happy about it.
<v Ira Glass>他告诉我，这感觉真的就像圣诞节的早晨。这些夹克正是他想要的，尽管他甚至不知道还有这种夹克存在。我不知道对艾米·谢尔曼-帕拉迪诺来说是否也有同样的感觉。你知道吗？卢克写给她的信，就像一份她甚至不知道自己想要、甚至不知道自己能想要的礼物，直到它送达。然后她真的为此感到很高兴。

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:58.550
<v Ira Glass>She's told people that she's kept Luke's letter in her desk all these years. It's been over a decade now. Well, today on our program, with the holidays happening, we have stories of people wanting stuff, daring to want stuff-- really, really feeling it. But getting the thing that you want can feel so much different than what you imagined. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Our show today in two acts-- Zoe Chace coming up in the second. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>她告诉别人，这些年来她一直把卢克的信放在桌子上。到现在已经过去十多年了。嗯，今天在我们的节目中，随着节日的到来，我们有关于人们想要东西、敢于想要东西的故事——真正地、深刻地感受着。但得到你想要的东西，感觉可能和你想象的截然不同。来自芝加哥WBEZ，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们今天的节目分为两幕——佐伊·蔡斯将在第二幕登场。留下来。

00:11:58.550 --> 00:12:07.810
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Lopsided Tannenbaum. Let's begin today with this Christmas story. It's a piece fiction read by the author, Maile Meloy.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，倾斜的圣诞树。让我们从这个圣诞故事开始。这是作者梅莉·梅洛伊朗读的一篇小说。

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:24.860
<v Maile Meloy>Everett thought it was a good tree. His four-year-old daughter agreed. His wife said it was lopsided and looked like a bush. It was a tall Douglas fir, bare on one side where it had crowded out its neighbor. The branchless side could go against the living room wall. The bushy side was for decorations.
<v Maile Meloy>埃弗雷特觉得这是一棵好树。他四岁的女儿表示同意。他妻子说它长歪了，看起来像个灌木丛。这是一棵高大的道格拉斯冷杉，靠邻居的那一面光秃秃的。没有树枝的那一面可以靠着客厅的墙。茂密的那一面用于装饰。

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:46.460
<v Maile Meloy>Everett dragged it through the snow by the trunk, and his daughter, Anne Marie clung to the upper branches and rode on her stomach. Pam, his wife, followed with an armload of pine boughs and juniper branches. She seemed to have decided not to say anything more about the tree, which was fine with Everett.
<v Maile Meloy>埃弗雷特拖着树干穿过积雪，他的女儿安妮·玛丽紧紧抓住上面的树枝，趴在上面。他的妻子帕姆带着一捧松枝和杜松枝跟在后面。她似乎决定不再对这棵树发表任何意见，这正合埃弗雷特的心意。

00:12:46.460 --> 00:13:04.430
<v Maile Meloy>The Jimmy was parked where the trail split off in the logging road, and Everett opened the back to throw the tools and boughs in, then rope the tree to the roof with nylon cords. Pam brushed off Anne Marie's snowsuit and buckled her in the front so she wouldn't get carsick. The smell of pine and juniper filled the car as they drove down the mountain.
<v Maile Meloy>吉米车停在小路和伐木路的分岔处，埃弗雷特打开后备箱把工具和树枝扔进去，然后用尼龙绳把树捆在车顶上。帕姆帮安妮·玛丽刷掉滑雪服上的雪，把她系在副驾驶座上，以免她晕车。当他们开车下山时，车里弥漫着松树和杜松的香味。

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:27.810
<v Maile Meloy>Everett sang "chestnut's roasting on an open fire" in his best lounge singer croon. "With Jack Frost nipping at your nose," he reached over and nipped at Anne Marie's and she squealed. He stopped, forgetting the words. Pam prompted-- "Yuletide carols," half singing, shy about her voice. Everett picked it up again with, "being sung by a choir."
<v Maile Meloy>埃弗雷特用他最好的休息室歌手的声音哼唱着《栗子在火上烤》。“当杰克·弗罗斯特咬你的鼻子时，”他伸出手捏了捏安妮·玛丽的鼻子，她尖叫起来。他停了下来，忘了词。帕姆提示道——“圣诞颂歌，”半唱着，对自己的声音感到害羞。埃弗雷特接着唱道，“正在由合唱团歌唱。”

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:51.300
<v Maile Meloy>That was when they saw the couple at the side of the road-- "folks dressed up like Eskimos." Everett thought for a second that he had conjured them up with his song. The man wore a blue parka and held up a broken crosscountry ski. The woman wore red gaiters over wool trousers, a man's peacoat, and a fur hat. They waved. Everett slowed to a stop and roll down the window.
<v Maile Meloy>就在那时，他们看到了路边的这对夫妇——“穿着像爱斯基摩人一样的人。”埃弗雷特有一秒钟以为是他的歌把他们召唤出来的。那个男人穿着蓝色派克大衣，举着一根断了的越野滑雪杖。女人穿着毛线裤外的红色绑腿，男式短大衣，戴着皮帽。他们挥手。埃弗雷特减速停下，摇下车窗。

00:13:51.300 --> 00:14:03.260
<v Maile Meloy>"Nice day for a ski," he said. "It was," the man said. He was about Everett's height and age, not yet pushing 40, with a day or two of bristle on his chin.
<v Maile Meloy>“滑雪的好日子，”他说。“曾经是，”那男人说。他大约和埃弗雷特身高和年龄相当，还没到40岁，下巴上有几天的胡茬。

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:12.140
<v Maile Meloy>"I broke a ski and we're lost," the woman began. "We're not lost," the man said. "We are completely lost," the woman said.
<v Maile Meloy>“我折断了滑雪杖，我们迷路了，”女人开始说。“我们没迷路，”男人说。“我们完全迷路了，”女人说。

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:29.630
<v Maile Meloy>She was younger than the man, with high, pink cheekbones in the cold. Everett felt friendly and warm from the tree and the singing. "Your car must be close," he said, "you're on the road." "The car is on a different road," the woman said. "Well, we'll find it," Everett said.
<v Maile Meloy>她比那个男人年轻，在寒冷中有着粉红色的高颧骨。因为这棵树和刚才的歌声，埃弗雷特感到友好而温暖。“你们的车一定很近，”他说，“你们在路上。”“车在另一条路上，”女人说。“好吧，我们会找到的，”埃弗雷特说。

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:43.250
<v Maile Meloy>In the rear view mirror, he saw Pam's eyes widen at him from the backseat. Pam was slight and dark-haired and accused him of favoring the kind of blonde who held sorority car washes. It was a joke, but it was partly true.
<v Maile Meloy>在后视镜里，他看到帕姆在后座上对他瞪大了眼睛。帕姆身材娇小，深色头发，总是指责他偏爱那种会举办联谊会洗车活动的金发女郎。这只是个玩笑，但部分是真实的。

00:14:43.250 --> 00:15:09.300
<v Maile Meloy>With a bucket and sponge, this hitchhiker would fit right in. Everett got out of the car and untied a nylon cord to open the back hatch. His wife had sleds and jackets in the back seat with her, and he thought she would want some separation of family and hitchhikers. She wouldn't look at him now.
<v Maile Meloy>带着水桶和海绵，这个搭车客会很合适。埃弗雷特下了车，解开一根尼龙绳打开后舱。他妻子后座有雪橇和夹克，他以为她会希望把家人和搭车客隔开。她现在不看他。

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:26.380
<v Maile Meloy>"You'll have to sit with the juniper boughs," he told the couple. "Better than freezing in a snowbank," the blonde said, climbing into the way back. Even in the wool pants, she had a sweet figure, of the car soaping type. "We really appreciate this," the man said.
<v Maile Meloy>“你们得坐在杜松枝上，”他告诉这对夫妇。“比冻在雪堆里好，”那个金发女郎说着，爬进后面。即使穿着羊毛裤，她也有个甜美的身材，是那种适合洗车的类型。“我们真的很感激，”男人说。

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:41.960
<v Maile Meloy>Everett shut them all in, lashed on the skis, and tied the tree down. It made no sense for Pam to be angry. This wasn't country where you left people in the snow. The man looked strong, but not too strong. Everett could take him if he needed to.
<v Maile Meloy>埃弗雷特关上车门，绑好滑雪板，系紧树。帕姆生气是没道理的。这又不是那种会把人丢在雪地里的地方。男人看起来很壮，但不是特别壮。必要时，埃弗雷特能对付他。

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:57.150
<v Maile Meloy>Back in the driver's seat, he pulled onto the road as snow fell in clumps off the big pine the couple had stood under. His daughter turned around in her seat as well as she could with her seat belt on and announced to the new passengers, "We have a CB radio."
<v Maile Meloy>回到驾驶座，他驶入公路，雪从这对夫妇站立的那棵大松树上簌簌落下。他女儿在座位上尽可能地转过身，向新乘客宣布：“我们有CB无线电。”

00:15:57.150 --> 00:16:06.730
<v Maile Meloy>"A CB?" the man in the parka said, "what's your handle?" Anne Marie looked confused. "Your name," Everett explained, "on the radio."
<v Maile Meloy>“CB？”穿派克大衣的男人说，“你的代号是什么？”安妮·玛丽看起来很困惑。“你的名字，”埃弗雷特解释道，“在无线电上的。”

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:25.500
<v Maile Meloy>"Batgirl," Anne Marie told the strangers, her cheeks flushing. "You've got a handle?" Everett asked the hitchhikers in back. "I'm Clyde," the man said. "Bonnie," the woman said. Everyone was silent for a moment.
<v Maile Meloy>“蝙蝠女孩，”安妮·玛丽告诉陌生人，脸颊发红。“你们有代号吗？”埃弗雷特问后面的搭车客。“我是克莱德，”男人说。“邦妮，”女人说。每个人沉默了片刻。

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:39.040
<v Maile Meloy>"That's really funny," Everett finally said. Though between his shoulder blades, he felt a prick of worry. "You must have a CB too." "No, those are our names," the man said.
<v Maile Meloy>“真好笑，”埃弗雷特最终说道。虽然在肩胛骨之间，他感到一阵担忧。“你们一定也有CB。”“不，那是我们的名字，”男人说。

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:53.530
<v Maile Meloy>The CB crackled on. "What's this Continental Divide?" a man's voice asked. Everett picked up the handset still thinking about Bonnie and Clyde. "You mean, what is it?" "Yeah," the voice said.
<v Maile Meloy>CB无线电吱吱作响。“什么是大陆分水岭？”一个男人的声音问。埃弗雷特拿起听筒，还在想邦妮和克莱德的事。“你是说，它是什么？”“是啊，”那个声音说。

00:16:53.530 --> 00:17:12.950
<v Maile Meloy>So Everett explained that the snow and rain on the west side of the mountains ran to the Pacific. And the water on the east side ran to the Gulf of Mexico. "I never heard of such a thing," the voice said. "That's what it is," Everett said. He thought of something, the recruiting of a witness.
<v Maile Meloy>于是埃弗雷特解释说，山脉西侧的雪和雨会流向太平洋。东侧的水流向墨西哥湾。“我从没听说过这种事，”那个声音说。“这就是它，”埃弗雷特说。他想到了什么，寻找证人。

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:27.050
<v Maile Meloy>"We just picked up some hitchhikers named Bonnie and Clyde," he said. "How about that?" A wheezing laugh came over the radio. "No kidding?" the voice asked. "You watch your back then. So long."
<v Maile Meloy>“我们刚接了两个搭车客，叫邦妮和克莱德，”他说。“怎么样？”无线电里传来一阵喘息般的笑声。“没开玩笑吧？”那个声音问。“那你小心点。再见。”

00:17:27.050 --> 00:18:05.620
<v Maile Meloy>Everett hung up the handset. "So," he said to his passengers, as if he hadn't just acted out of fear of them, "whereas your stolen jalopy?" "We parked by Fire Creek," Clyde said. Everett could tell he wasn't from Montana. If he'd been from Montana, he would have said "crick." "You didn't get far," he said. "No," Bonnie said. "How'd you break the ski?" Bonnie and Clyde both fell silent.
<v Maile Meloy>埃弗雷特挂上听筒。“那么，”他对着乘客说，就好像他刚才没有因为害怕他们而采取行动，“你们被偷的旧车在哪？”“我们停在火溪边，”克莱德说。埃弗雷特看得出他不是蒙大拿人。如果是蒙大拿人，他会说“crick”。“你们没走多远，”他说。“没有，”邦妮说。“你们怎么弄断滑雪杖的？”邦妮和克莱德都沉默了。

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:40.160
<v Maile Meloy>Everett drove. The windows were iced from everyone's breathing, and he turned up the defroster. The fans seemed very loud. He took the road Fire Creek. "This is it," he said stopping the Jimmy. There was a place at the trail had to park cars, but there were no cars, just snow and trees and the creek running under the ice. Everett didn't look back at his life. He scanned the empty turn out and hoped this was not one of those times you looked back on and wished he had done one thing different, though it seem perfectly natural to do what you did at the time.
<v Maile Meloy>埃弗雷特开车。窗户因为呼吸而结了冰，他调高了除霜器。风扇声显得很大。他走火溪路。“就是这，”他停下吉米车说。小径边有停车的地方，但没有车，只有雪和树，还有冰下的溪流。埃弗雷特没有回头看自己的生活。他扫视着空荡荡的出口，希望这不会是那种回头看时，会希望自己做点不同选择的时候，尽管当时做那件事似乎完全自然。

00:18:40.160 --> 00:19:04.910
<v Maile Meloy>"Where's the car?" Bonnie asked. "This is where we parked," Clyde said. They were genuinely surprised and Everett almost laughed with relief. There was no con, no ambush. He untied the rope and the couple climbed out and walked to where their car had been. Bonnie's arm brushed against Everett's when she passed, but he didn't think she meant it. She was thinking about the missing car.
<v Maile Meloy>“车呢？”邦妮问。“我们停在这，”克莱德说。他们真的很惊讶，埃弗雷特几乎如释重负地笑了起来。没有诈骗，没有伏击。他解开绳子，这对夫妇下了车，走向他们车子本来的位置。邦妮路过时手臂擦过了埃弗雷特，但他觉得她不是故意的。她正在想那辆失踪的车。

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:17.740
<v Maile Meloy>He got in the Jimmy to let them discuss it. Pam reached into the way back to pull the saw and the axe from under the boughs Clyde and Bonnie had been sitting on, and she tucked the tools under her feet.
<v Maile Meloy>他回到吉米车上，让他们讨论。帕姆伸手进后舱，从克莱德和邦妮坐过的树枝下抽出锯子和斧头，把工具塞在脚下。

00:19:17.740 --> 00:19:46.690
<v Maile Meloy>"What are we doing with these people in our car?" she asked. "Can't leave people in the snow," he said. "We have a child, Everett." "And," he said, with the confidence he had just now recovered, "we're showing her that you don't leave people in the snow. Right, Anne Marie?" "Right," Anne Marie said, but she watched them both. Pam gave Everett a dark, unforgiving stare.
<v Maile Meloy>“我们要带这些人在车里做什么？”她问。“不能把人丢在雪地里，”他说。“我们有孩子，埃弗雷特。”“而且，”他用刚恢复的自信说，“我们在教她，不能把人丢在雪地里。对吧，安妮·玛丽？”“对，”安妮·玛丽说，但她盯着他们俩。帕姆给了埃弗雷特一个阴沉、不可原谅的眼神。

00:19:46.690 --> 00:20:02.070
<v Maile Meloy>Outside in the snow, Bonnie and Clyde's voices rose a notch. "You said we could leave the keys in it," Bonnie said. "You said this was Montana, and that's what people do." "That is what they do," Clyde said. "Then who stole our car?"
<v Maile Meloy>外面的雪地里，邦妮和克莱德的声音高了一个调。“你说我们可以把钥匙留在车里的，”邦妮说。“你说这是蒙大拿，人们都这么做。”“他们确实这么做，”克莱德说。“那谁偷了我们的车？”

00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:24.960
<v Maile Meloy>Snow off the trees drifted around them, and the two stood staring at each other for a minute. Then Bonnie started to laugh. She had a throaty, movie star laugh that rose into a series of uncontrolled giggles. Her husband shook his head at her in exasperation. Everett felt the opposite. He liked her even more-- a woman who could laugh at her own stolen car and who looked like that when she did it.
<v Maile Meloy>树上的积雪在他们周围飘落，两人互相对视了一分钟。然后邦妮开始笑。她有一种喉音的、电影明星般的笑声，演变成一阵无法控制的咯咯笑。她丈夫懊恼地摇着头。埃弗雷特感觉恰恰相反。他更喜欢她了——一个能拿自己被偷的车开玩笑，而且笑起来还那么好看的女人。

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:46.500
<v Maile Meloy>She was still laughing when they started back to the car. "You ask for a ride," she told her husband, her voice not lowered enough. Ever looked to Pam in the backseat. Pam frowned, then nodded. He got out of the Jimmy, and this time Bonnie did brush his arm on purpose. He was sure of it.
<v Maile Meloy>当他们走回车边时，她还在笑。“你要求搭车，”她对丈夫说，声音压得不够低。埃弗雷特看向后座的帕姆。帕姆皱了皱眉，然后点点头。他下了吉米车，这一次邦妮确实故意擦过了他的手臂。他确定。

00:20:46.500 --> 00:21:17.010
<v Maile Meloy>When she and Clyde were bundled in the way back again with the tree tied down, Everett called in the theft of the car on the CB. "Do you think we should wait for the cops," Clyde asked. "I'm not waiting in the cold anymore," Bonnie said. "Jesus, who steels a car at Christmas?" Clyde said, "People do all kinds of things at Christmas." No one had any response to that.
