<v Maile Meloy>帕姆从壁炉架转过身。当她发现他在注视自己时，脸上有时会浮现出一种有趣、讽刺的微笑，一种成熟的微笑，既自信又自嘲。但现在她看起来挑衅而年轻。帕姆双手叉腰。

00:33:05.620 --> 00:33:29.840
<v Maile Meloy>"Look, if you want to go get them, just go." "They'll have gone by now," he said, with a catch in his voice. Pam threw the burnt matches into the fire. In the kitchen, she put the matchbook in the kitchen drawer, then she dialed the phone, watching Everett as if waiting for him to stop her. "I called earlier about the couple with the stolen car," she said in her business-like phone voice. "Are they still there?"
<v Maile Meloy>“听着，如果你想去接他们，就去吧。”“他们现在肯定已经走了，”他声音哽咽地说。帕姆把烧焦的火柴扔进火里。在厨房，她把火柴盒放进抽屉，然后拨了电话，注视着埃弗雷特，仿佛在等他阻止自己。“我之前打过电话，关于那对车子被偷的夫妇，”她用那种公事公办的语气说。“他们还在吗？”

00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:52.520
<v Maile Meloy>She waited, looking out the dark, glass door she had locked against him. "Hi, Bonnie," she said into the phone. "It's Pam from the car. We picked you up. Hi." Her laugh sounded social, but Everett could hear the nervousness in it. "No, I don't think I introduced myself. Do you still want to help with the tree? Everett could run down and get you."
<v Maile Meloy>她等着，望着她曾为他锁上的黑暗玻璃门。“嗨，邦妮，”她对着电话说。“我是车上的帕姆。我们接你们的。嗨。”她的笑声听起来很社交，但埃弗雷特能听出其中的紧张。“不，我想我还没自我介绍。你们还想帮忙装饰圣诞树吗？埃弗雷特可以跑一趟去接你们。”

00:33:52.520 --> 00:34:29.600
<v Maile Meloy>She paused, listening. "Put Clyde on," she said, and she turned away from Everett. He watched the curve of his wife's ass as she leaned on the kitchen counter, lifting her right foot and nervously tapping the toe on the floor. "Clyde," she said, "please come up for dinner. Anne Marie would love to show off the tree. Really, we'd love it. Good, he'll be right down." She hung up the phone and turned to Everett. "Merry Christmas," she said.
<v Maile Meloy>她停下来听。“让克莱德接电话，”她说，然后背对着埃弗雷特。他看着妻子靠在厨台上时臀部的曲线，抬起右脚紧张地用脚尖敲击地面。“克莱德，”她说，“请过来吃晚餐吧。安妮·玛丽会很高兴展示圣诞树的。真的，我们很期待。好的，他马上就来。”她挂断电话转向埃弗雷特。“圣诞快乐，”她说。

00:34:29.600 --> 00:34:55.420
<v Maile Meloy>He was not sure how to behave. Anne Marie was still decorating the lower branches of the tree. "So," Pam said. She stirred the pot on the stove with a wooden spoon, "Do you want to go get them?" Everett pushed himself out of the chair. "Want to come along, Anne Marie?" he asked. His daughter looked up at him. "Are you going to get those people?" "Yes," he said, "to help with the tree."
<v Maile Meloy>他不知道该怎么表现。安妮·玛丽还在装饰圣诞树的下层树枝。“那么，”帕姆说。她用木勺搅拌炉子上的锅，“你想去接他们吗？”埃弗雷特推开椅子起身。“想一起去吗，安妮·玛丽？”他问。女儿抬头看着他。“你要去接那些人吗？”“是的，”他说，“来帮忙装饰圣诞树。”

00:34:55.420 --> 00:35:22.980
<v Maile Meloy>Anne Marie nodded, untangling the loop of string on a tiny ukulele. "I'll stay here," she said. He kissed his wife goodbye on the top of her head. Was she attracted to Clyde? He wanted to take off her clothes right now and see. He was conscious of his own breathing, and he could tell she was unsteady. "It's Christmastime," he said. "I'll be right back."
<v Maile Meloy>安妮·玛丽点点头，解开一个小尤克里里上的弦。“我留在这里，”她说。他亲吻了妻子的头顶告别。她真的被克莱德吸引了吗？他现在就想脱掉她的衣服看看。他能感觉到自己的呼吸，也能看出她很不稳定。“现在是圣诞节，”他说。“我马上回来。”

00:35:22.980 --> 00:35:38.580
<v Maile Meloy>He went out into the cold air. The Jimmy started up easily, and he had it in low gear down the hill toward town. He wanted to decide as he drove what they were doing. He wanted to separate his impulse to be a good Samaritan from the kiss on the corner of his mouth.
<v Maile Meloy>他走进寒冷的空气中。吉米车很容易就发动了，他挂低档向山下的镇子开去。他想在开车时决定他们在做什么。他想把善良救助的冲动与嘴角的那个吻分开。

00:35:38.580 --> 00:36:02.880
<v Maile Meloy>Bonnie did not, he was fairly sure, just want to hang angels on a tree. Clyde's asking her to move in with his mistress had put her in a giddy, reckless mood, and Everett was the beneficiary. He wasn't going to think about Clyde's low, sincere apology to Pam or about Pam turning away on the phone to ask Clyde to come to the house. Although he found he wanted very much to think about that.
<v Maile Meloy>他相当肯定，邦妮不仅仅是想在圣诞树上挂天使。克莱德要求她搬进他情妇的小屋，这让她处于一种轻浮、鲁莽的情绪中，而埃弗雷特是受益者。他不想去想克莱德对帕姆的那些低沉、真诚的道歉，或者帕姆在电话里转身请克莱德到家里来。尽管他发现自己非常想去想那些。

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:21.780
<v Maile Meloy>He thought instead about Anne Marie and how the evening might work out for her. The lesson about not abandoning people was a good one. The silent, submerged unhappiness of the evening couldn't be good for a kid. And now it was gone, dissolved by Pam's call into the buzz of unsettled excitement.
<v Maile Meloy>他转而想到安妮·玛丽，想到这个夜晚可能会对她产生什么影响。关于不遗弃人的教训是很好的。沉默、压抑的夜晚对孩子不好。现在，一切都消失了，被帕姆的电话溶解进不安的兴奋中。

00:36:21.780 --> 00:36:51.870
<v Maile Meloy>The streets were dark and empty, the houses warm with light. He wanted to keep thinking, but he was at the station before he'd sorted things out. And Bonnie was waiting on the curb. She climbed into the front seat and kicked the snow off her boots. "Hi," she said, and she clutched her hands in her lap. She shuddered once from nervousness or cold. "Clyde'll be here in a second," she said, "he's signing something about the car." "OK," he said.
<v Maile Meloy>街道黑暗而空旷，房屋在灯光中温暖。他想继续思考，但在梳理清楚之前就到了警局。邦妮在路边等候。她爬上副驾驶座，踢掉靴子上的雪。“嗨，”她说，把手绞在一起放在膝盖上。因为紧张或寒冷，她颤抖了一下。“克莱德马上就来，”她说，“他在签关于车子的东西。”“好的，”他说。

00:36:51.870 --> 00:37:20.280
<v Maile Meloy>She looked at Everett and seemed about to say something, and then she was in his arms. He gathered her up as well as he could given her thick coat and the awkward position and kissed her sweet face. Her cheeks were cold, but her lips were warm, and she was trembling. The peacoat was unbuttoned and he reached inside to feel the curve of her breast through her sweater. A second later, they pulled apart and Bonnie smoothed her hair.
<v Maile Meloy>她看着埃弗雷特，似乎想说什么，然后投入了他的怀抱。考虑到厚重的外套和尴尬的位置，他尽可能地拥抱她，亲吻她甜美的脸庞。她的脸颊冰冷，但嘴唇温暖，她正在颤抖。短大衣没扣扣子，他伸手进去，透过毛衣感受她乳房的曲线。一秒钟后，他们分开，邦妮梳理头发。

00:37:20.280 --> 00:37:59.110
<v Maile Meloy>The lighted glass door of the police station opened, and Clyde walked with his long stride toward them and got in the backseat. Everett thought there must be a smell in the car from the kiss, an electricity. But the husband said nothing, and Everett drove the outlaws back to his house. They talked about the stolen car, and the cold, and the tree. All the while, Everett felt both the threat of disorder and the steady, thrumming promise of having everything he wanted all at once.
<v Maile Meloy>警察局亮灯的玻璃门开了，克莱德迈着大步向他们走来，钻进后座。埃弗雷特以为车里一定会有那种亲吻留下的气味，那种电流感。但丈夫什么也没说，埃弗雷特载着这些亡命徒回到家。他们谈论失窃的车、寒冷和树。一路上，埃弗雷特既感受到了混乱的威胁，也感受到了同时拥有想要的一切的那种稳定、悸动的希望。

00:37:59.110 --> 00:38:04.390
<v Ira Glass>Maile Meloy. This story appears in her book, Both Ways is the Only Way I Want It.
<v Ira Glass>梅莉·梅洛伊。这个故事出现在她的书《两种方式是我想要的唯一方式》中。

00:38:04.390 --> 00:38:32.370
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "MAKE YOU BETTER" BY THE DECEMBERISTS"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 乐队The Decemberists的《让你要更好》]

00:38:32.370 --> 00:38:44.870
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, why some Republican leaders in North Carolina are feeling weird about Santa Claus this year. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，为什么北卡罗来纳州的一些共和党领袖今年对圣诞老人感到怪怪的。稍后从芝加哥公共广播电台继续我们的节目。

00:38:44.870 --> 00:39:19.550
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Our program today, Just What I Wanted. We've arrived at Act Two of our program, Act Two. He's making a list, checking it nice. Gonna find out who voted twice. So this fall, what certain Republicans thought they would discover under the tree was proof of voter fraud. This, of course, is one of the big divisive issues that separate the Right and the Left in our country. Democrats in the mainstream media point out that there is no evidence of significant voter fraud happening in our elections. Republicans say that it easily could be happening, given the way our elections are run, and we do not have the tools to track it.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们今天的节目，《恰如我愿》。我们已经来到节目的第二幕，第二幕。他列了个清单，核对得很仔细。要找出谁投了两次票。所以今年秋天，某些共和党人以为在圣诞树下会发现的是选民欺诈的证据。当然，这是分裂我们国家右派和左派的重大问题之一。民主党人和主流媒体指出，没有证据表明在我们的选举中存在重大的选民欺诈。共和党人说，考虑到我们选举的运作方式，这种情况很容易发生，而且我们没有跟踪它的工具。

00:39:19.550 --> 00:39:33.260
<v Ira Glass>And this year, the place where the search has been the most intense for voter fraud was North Carolina. And this time, Zoe Chace reports, Republicans really had moments when they thought they had found proof of organized fraud.
<v Ira Glass>而今年，北卡罗来纳州是选民欺诈搜查最激烈的地方。而这一次，据佐伊·蔡斯报道，共和党人确实有那么一刻以为他们发现了组织欺诈的证据。

00:39:33.260 --> 00:39:48.440
<v Zoe Chace>The man leading the hunt for voter fraud in North Carolina is Dallas Woodhouse, executive director of the North Carolina Republican Party. Picture a Southern party boss. It's probably Dallas-- white guy in an Oxford shirt, bottom buttons undone, loafers, no socks--
<v Zoe Chace>北卡罗来纳州选民欺诈调查的负责人是达拉斯·伍德豪斯，北卡罗来纳州共和党执行董事。想象一个南方政党老板。很可能是达拉斯——穿着牛津衬衫，下面没扣扣子，没穿袜子的乐福鞋——

00:39:48.440 --> 00:39:49.640
<v Zoe Chace>Are you really smoking a cigar right now?
<v Zoe Chace>你现在真的在抽雪茄吗？

00:39:49.640 --> 00:39:50.400
<v Dallas Woodhouse>I am.
<v Dallas Woodhouse>我是。

00:39:50.400 --> 00:40:06.260
<v Zoe Chace>--a cigar in the corner of his mouth. Younger and handsomer than you're imagining, I think. He used to be a TV anchor. He's trying to cut back on cigars since the election ended. I point out there have been a lot of studies looking into voter fraud that conclude voter fraud is minuscule, almost nonexistent.
<v Zoe Chace>——嘴边叼着雪茄。比你们想象的更年轻、更帅气。他曾是电视主播。选举结束后他正试图少抽雪茄。我指出，已经有很多研究调查选民欺诈，结论是选民欺诈微乎其微，几乎不存在。

00:40:06.260 --> 00:40:09.630
<v Zoe Chace>I think that if you see any study, there is no study that ever points to--
<v Zoe Chace>我想如果你看任何研究，没有研究会指出——

00:40:09.630 --> 00:40:10.000
<v Dallas Woodhouse>You know, look, look.
<v Dallas Woodhouse>你知道，看，看。

00:40:10.000 --> 00:40:12.110
<v Zoe Chace>Look, I've got to say this.
<v Zoe Chace>看，我得说这个。

00:40:12.110 --> 00:40:21.710
<v Dallas Woodhouse>Don't show me studies. Academics, I mean, a bunch of knuckleheads, pointy-headed professors. We deal in the real world.
<v Dallas Woodhouse>别给我看研究。那些学者，我是说，一群蠢蛋，尖头教授。我们处理的是现实世界。

00:40:21.710 --> 00:40:43.460
<v Zoe Chace>In the real world, Dallas looks for voter fraud, he will tell you, as a matter of principle. There was also a numbers game going on this November. The Republican governor of North Carolina refused to concede the election for weeks because it was such a close race. At the moment I met Dallas, the governor was down just 8,500 votes out of 4.7 million. There were other close races also.
<v Zoe Chace>在现实世界中，达拉斯会告诉你，他寻找选民欺诈是基于原则。今年11月还有一个数字游戏。北卡罗来纳州的共和党州长几周来拒绝承认选举结果，因为竞争太激烈。我和达拉斯见面时，州长在470万张选票中落后仅8500票。还有其他激烈的竞争。

00:40:43.460 --> 00:40:55.720
<v Zoe Chace>Dallas was out running down every claim of voter fraud around the state. His team accused people of voting twice. That was dismissed. People who voted when they were dead, that was dismissed. Felons who voted.
<v Zoe Chace>达拉斯一直在追查全州所有选民欺诈的指控。他的团队指控有人投了两次票。被驳回了。死人投票，被驳回了。重罪犯投票。

00:40:55.720 --> 00:41:11.750
<v Dallas Woodhouse>Yeah, but we made a challenge. It ended up not being correct, of a person who showed up on the felon list. And we found somebody that voted, had the same first name, same last name, same birth date. It turns out it wasn't the same person. It seems like to me you have to make an attempt to address that.
<v Dallas Woodhouse>是啊，但我们提出了质疑。最终证明是不正确的，有人出现在重罪犯名单上。我们发现有人投票，名字相同，姓氏相同，出生日期相同。结果发现不是同一个人。在我看来，你必须尝试去解决这个问题。

00:41:11.750 --> 00:41:18.680
<v Zoe Chace>Dallas found 24 ineligible felons out of 43 they accused. He's not perfect.
<v Zoe Chace>达拉斯在他们指控的43人中发现了24名不合格的重罪犯。他不完美。

00:41:18.680 --> 00:41:25.550
<v Dallas Woodhouse>Look, I'm not-- the only perfect person I know walked on water, and he's my Savior, Jesus Christ. We are not perfect.
<v Dallas Woodhouse>看，我不是——我知道唯一完美的人是曾经在水上行走的那位，他是我的救主耶稣基督。我们不完美。

00:41:25.550 --> 00:41:36.210
<v Zoe Chace>The day we talk, Dallas has a smoking gun, a few hundred ballots in the southeast of the state, lots with the same handwriting on them. Jackpot. He takes a breath.
<v Zoe Chace>我们谈话的那天，达拉斯有了确凿证据，该州东南部有几百张选票，很多笔迹相同。大奖。他深吸了一口气。

00:41:36.210 --> 00:41:43.610
<v Dallas Woodhouse>They do realize that 300 or 400 write-in votes can't be done by one person. They do realize that's illegal.
<v Dallas Woodhouse>他们确实意识到300或400张填写票不可能由一个人完成。他们确实意识到那是违法的。

00:41:43.610 --> 00:42:04.820
<v Zoe Chace>Here's what happened. Down in Bladen County, North Carolina, this guy Brian was going through absentee balance at the Board of Elections. He's a board member, a Republican. His job was to scrutinize each ballot as it came out of the machine. He's heard a lot about voter fraud, never seen it before, but he believes it's widespread. He's heard a lot about it on the news. His eyes are peeled.
<v Zoe Chace>发生的事情是这样的。在北卡罗来纳州的布雷登县，这个叫布莱恩的家伙正在选举委员会审查缺席选票。他是一名董事会成员，共和党人。他的工作是审查从机器里出来的每一张选票。他听说过很多关于选民欺诈的事，以前从没见过，但他相信它很普遍。他在新闻里听说过很多。他瞪大了眼睛。

00:42:04.820 --> 00:42:17.780
<v Zoe Chace>And then he notices something. Way down at the bottom of the ballot, there was room for a write-in candidate, the county soil and water district supervisor. And he kept seeing the name Franklin Graham.
<v Zoe Chace>然后他注意到了一些东西。在选票的最下方，有一个填写候选人的空格，是县土壤和水资源区主管的。他一直看到“富兰克林·格雷厄姆”这个名字。

00:42:17.780 --> 00:42:20.600
<v Brian>And it looked like the same handwriting a lot of times.
<v Brian>而且看起来很多时候笔迹都一样。

00:42:20.600 --> 00:42:23.360
<v Zoe Chace>He demonstrates by writing the name in my notebook.
<v Zoe Chace>他通过在我的笔记本上写下这个名字来演示。

00:42:23.360 --> 00:42:34.950
<v Brian>Every time I write this, my R's are going to be the same. My A's are pretty much going to be the same. My K's are going to be the same. So if I write it again as you watch.
<v Brian>每次我写这个时，我的R都会一样。我的A基本上都会一样。我的K基本上都会一样。所以如果我再写一次，看着。

00:42:34.950 --> 00:42:38.530
<v Zoe Chace>Franklin Graham.
<v Zoe Chace>富兰克林·格雷厄姆。

00:42:38.530 --> 00:42:41.560
<v Brian>You see similarities between certain letters every time.
<v Brian>你每次都能看到某些字母之间的相似之处。

00:42:41.560 --> 00:42:43.170
<v Zoe Chace>Like how many times?
<v Zoe Chace>有多少次？

00:42:43.170 --> 00:42:49.870
<v Brian>Considerable, I would say, I don't, 50 to 100.
<v Brian>相当多，我会说，我不知道，50到100次。

00:42:49.870 --> 00:42:54.510
<v Zoe Chace>Of one person putting in 100 votes.
<v Zoe Chace>一个人投了100票。

00:42:54.510 --> 00:42:56.230
<v Brian>You could infer that.
<v Brian>你可以推断出这一点。

00:42:56.230 --> 00:43:10.990
<v Zoe Chace>In all, maybe 300 ballots. Brian is a paramedic. He's used to looking at EKG waveforms. He says he has an eye for patterns like the way someone writes their name. Brian is also pretty careful. He wasn't sure it was fraud. But it looked suspicious.
<v Zoe Chace>总共可能有300张选票。布莱恩是一名护理人员。他习惯看心电图波形。他说他对模式有眼光，比如别人写名字的方式。布莱恩也很谨慎。他不确定那是否是欺诈。但看起来很可疑。

00:43:10.990 --> 00:43:14.770
<v Brian>I wouldn't infer that, because obviously I'm not an expert. But you could infer that.
<v Brian>我不会那样推断，因为显然我不是专家。但你可以推断出这一点。

00:43:14.770 --> 00:43:17.390
<v Zoe Chace>Dallas Woodhouse does more than infer.
<v Zoe Chace>达拉斯·伍德豪斯做的不仅仅是推断。

00:43:17.390 --> 00:43:31.390
<v Dallas Woodhouse>Come on. You cannot look at me in a straight face and think the same handwriting puts in several hundred votes for one person and not call that fraud. That is the definition of fraud.
<v Dallas Woodhouse>得了吧。你不可能看着我的脸，认为同一个人为某人投了几百票，而不称之为欺诈。这正是欺诈的定义。

00:43:31.390 --> 00:44:00.010
<v Zoe Chace>It wasn't just one person's handwriting. It was around a dozen people. Dallas has a term for what he thinks this is. He calls it an absentee ballot mill. This is what he pictures when he hears about all these ballots with the same handwriting on them. Key Democratic operatives, running around searching for old people who barely know their own names and then filling out ballots for them. A blatant scheme, Dallas thinks, that gins up votes for the Democrats. And the Democratic governor, remember, is only up by a couple thousand votes.
<v Zoe Chace>那不仅仅是一个人的笔迹。大约有十几个人。达拉斯对这件事有一个称呼。他称之为“缺席选票工厂”。当他听到所有这些笔迹相同的选票时，他想象的就是这个画面。民主党的关键操盘手四处寻找那些几乎连自己名字都不知道的老人，然后为他们填写选票。达拉斯认为这是一个明目张胆的计划，为民主党赚取选票。记住，那位民主党州长只领先了几千票。

00:44:00.010 --> 00:44:21.360
<v Dallas Woodhouse>Should the election board find that these are absentee ballot mills, with the purpose of fraudulent voting, those people should go to jail. They should go to jail. They should spend the first term of the Trump administration behind bars.
<v Dallas Woodhouse>如果选举委员会发现这些是缺席选票工厂，目的是进行欺诈性投票，这些人就应该坐牢。他们应该坐牢。他们应该在特朗普政府的第一个任期里待在监狱里。

00:44:21.360 --> 00:44:30.920
<v Zoe Chace>I go down south to meet the victims of the alleged scheme, the democratic voters of Bladen County. It is an unusual interviewing situation.
<v Zoe Chace>我去了南方，去见据称是该计划的受害者，布雷登县的民主党选民。这是一种不寻常的采访情况。

00:44:30.920 --> 00:44:31.620
<v Man 1>This is Zoe.
<v Man 1>这是佐伊。

00:44:31.620 --> 00:44:32.940
<v Zoe Chace>Hi.
<v Zoe Chace>嗨。

00:44:32.940 --> 00:44:36.520
<v Man 1>She's out of New York. She has a talk radio show.
<v Man 1>她是纽约来的。她有一个谈话广播节目。

00:44:36.520 --> 00:44:55.460
<v Zoe Chace>I'm at a senior center in this classroom. I'm standing up at the front with a microphone like a teacher. There are rows of older people sitting quietly, staring back at me. Their dominoes game is sealed up in a Tupperware. I go from person to person and ask each one about their vote while everyone else silently watches. It's awkward.
<v Zoe Chace>我站在老人中心的一间教室里。我像老师一样站在前面拿着麦克风。一排排的老年人安静地坐着，盯着我看。他们的多米诺骨牌游戏被封在特百惠盒子里。我从一个人走到另一个人，每个人都在沉默地看着的情况下问每个人的投票情况。很尴尬。

00:44:55.460 --> 00:44:59.040
<v Zoe Chace>May I ask you about your vote? OK.
<v Zoe Chace>我可以问问你的投票吗？好的。

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:17.550
<v Ira Glass>Her name is Lula Pearl Graham. She did vote absentee this year. She says someone helped her get an absentee ballot in. This person worked with a political PAC, the Bladen County Improvement Association PAC. And she followed their advice on how to vote. They gave her a sample ballot with a slate of endorsed candidates.
<v Ira Glass>她叫卢拉·珀尔·格雷厄姆。她今年确实投了缺席选票。她说有人帮她拿到了一张缺席选票。这个人与一个政治行动委员会（PAC）合作，即布雷登县改进协会PAC。她听从了他们关于如何投票的建议。他们给了她一张样本选票，上面列有支持的候选人名单。

00:45:17.550 --> 00:45:23.130
<v Zoe Chace>Did you know everybody who you were voting for on the ballot this year?
<v Zoe Chace>你今年知道选票上你要投给谁吗？

00:45:23.130 --> 00:45:25.470
<v Lula Pearl Graham>No, I didn't.
<v Lula Pearl Graham>不，不知道。

00:45:25.470 --> 00:45:28.760
<v Zoe Chace>But did you know who you were voting for for governor?
<v Zoe Chace>但你知道你要投给州长吗？

00:45:28.760 --> 00:45:29.300
<v Lula Pearl Graham>Yeah.
<v Lula Pearl Graham>是的。

00:45:29.300 --> 00:45:34.680
<v Zoe Chace>OK. What about for soil and water? Can I ask you who you voted for there? Do you know?
<v Zoe Chace>好的。那么土壤和水资源呢？我可以问问你投给了谁吗？你知道吗？

00:45:34.680 --> 00:45:36.140
<v Lula Pearl Graham>I don't remember.
<v Lula Pearl Graham>我不记得了。

00:45:36.140 --> 00:45:41.070
<v Zoe Chace>But do you trust their recommendations? The PAC with the sample ballot and everything?
<v Zoe Chace>但你信任他们的推荐吗？那个带有样本选票的PAC等等？

00:45:41.070 --> 00:45:43.810
<v Lula Pearl Graham>Oh, yes, they're good. They're good.
<v Lula Pearl Graham>哦，是的，他们很好。他们很好。

00:45:43.810 --> 00:45:44.620
<v Zoe Chace>Why?
<v Zoe Chace>为什么？

00:45:44.620 --> 00:45:56.010
<v Lula Pearl Graham>Because when you don't know the people, they'll tell you-- they explain them to you. So you say, well, I would like to vote here, but I don't know who this is. They tell you.
<v Lula Pearl Graham>因为当你不知道这些人时，他们会告诉你——他们为你解释。所以你会说，嗯，我想投这里，但我不知道这是谁。他们会告诉你。

00:45:56.010 --> 00:46:29.640
<v Zoe Chace>What she's describing doesn't sound illegal. If the PAC bought her vote, it would be illegal. If the PAC threatened her, it would be illegal. Giving someone a slate of candidates to vote for is not illegal. Having someone assist you in filling out your ballot is not illegal. Though if you do assist someone, you're supposed to give them some space while they actually vote and then sign the envelope saying you assisted them. But I do think if Dallas, the Republican guy, chewed his cigar into this room and heard Lula Pearl talk, he would be horrified, based on how he thinks about this stuff.
<v Zoe Chace>她所描述的听起来并不违法。如果PAC买了她的选票，那是违法的。如果PAC威胁她，那是违法的。给某人一张候选人名单投票不是违法的。让某人协助你填写选票不是违法的。虽然如果你确实协助某人，你应该在他们实际投票时给他们一些空间，然后签署信封声明你协助了他们。但我确实认为，如果达拉斯，那个共和党人，咀嚼着雪茄走进这个房间，听到卢拉·珀尔这么说，基于他对此事的看法，他会感到震惊。

00:46:29.640 --> 00:46:45.540
<v Dallas Woodhouse>Under a select few circumstances, you can get assistance from a relative or somebody who declares your help. Now, that is intended to be a neighbor helping a neighbor, a friend helping a friend. If somebody is going out there and helping hundreds of people, they are harvesting ballots.
<v Dallas Woodhouse>在极少数情况下，你可以从亲戚或某人那里获得协助，声明你是需要帮助的。现在，那本来应该是邻里互助、朋友互助。如果有人出去帮助成百上千的人，他们就是在“收割选票”。

00:46:45.540 --> 00:47:17.440
<v Zoe Chace>For Dallas, Lula Pearl saying she doesn't know anything about the candidates she's voting for but votes for them anyway, at the suggestion of volunteers who get $35 a day reimbursement for gas and food, that's shady to Dallas on its face. If it's not technically illegal, it should be. It's supposed to be one person, one vote. As soon as you have one person helping lots and lots of people vote, it feels like a scheme to him. Lula Pearl sees it differently. Having a volunteer come to the house makes it easier for her to vote. She wants it.
<v Zoe Chace>对于达拉斯来说，卢拉·珀尔说她对投票的候选人一无所知，但仍然投票，是在那些每天获得35美元汽油和食物补贴的志愿者的建议下完成的，这在达拉斯看来显然是可疑的。如果从技术上讲不违法，也应该违法。应该是“一人一票”。一旦你有一个人帮助很多人投票，对他来说就像是一个阴谋。卢拉·珀尔的看法不同。有一个志愿者来家里让她投票更容易。她想要这样。

00:47:17.440 --> 00:47:36.370
<v Lula Pearl Graham>I don't drive, and I have two knee implants. Sometimes it's difficult to get around. So somebody would come and pick me up. But sometimes I don't feel like going that way. So I just let someone come and vote for me and let-- come and let me fill out the application. And then it goes, and it comes back.
<v Lula Pearl Graham>我不开车，而且我做了两次膝盖植入手术。有时候走动很困难。所以有人会来接我。但有时候我不想那样去。所以我就让人来帮我投，让人——来让我填写申请表。然后它寄出，再回来。

00:47:36.370 --> 00:47:41.140
<v Zoe Chace>Voting is very important to her. She feels like, why shouldn't it be easy?
<v Zoe Chace>投票对她来说非常重要。她觉得，为什么不简单点呢？

00:47:41.140 --> 00:47:50.410
<v Lula Pearl Graham>My mother and my father, they marched all the way from Selma, Alabama to Birmingham, Alabama for the right for me to vote. And I'm going to vote.
<v Lula Pearl Graham>我的母亲和父亲，他们为了我投票的权利，从阿拉巴马州的塞尔玛一直游行到阿拉巴马州的伯明翰。我会去投票的。

00:47:50.410 --> 00:48:04.050
<v Zoe Chace>This is, in fact, why this organization that helped her with the ballot exists, the Bladen County Improvement Association PAC. It exists to protect the vote for black people in the community.
<v Zoe Chace>事实上，这就是那个帮她投票的组织——布雷登县改进协会PAC存在的原因。它的存在是为了保护社区中黑人的投票权。

00:48:04.050 --> 00:48:27.780
<v Zoe Chace>The head of the PAC is Horace Munn. Horace Munn-- for a man allegedly at the center of a blatant scheme to rig an election-- Horace is pretty easy to find. Like, I couldn't get any service on my phone driving down to Bladen County, so I went into this convenience store at the side of the road. Tammy let me use her phone. She goes, oh, you're talking to Horace? That's my godfather. Let me have the phone. Horace, where do you want me to send her?
<v Zoe Chace>PAC的负责人是霍勒斯·曼恩。霍勒斯·曼恩——对于一个据称处于操纵选举阴谋中心的家伙——霍勒斯其实很容易找到。就像，我开车去布雷登县时手机没信号，所以我就进了路边这家便利店。塔米让我用了她的电话。她说，哦，你要和霍勒斯说话？那是我教父。把电话给我。霍勒斯，你想让我把她送到哪？

00:48:27.780 --> 00:48:43.950
<v Zoe Chace>He was at the town hall in East Arcadia. He's on the town council. He's the fire chief. Horace is a former soldier, a paratrooper, got the Bronze Star in the First Gulf War. He uses that old civil rights era language like, I fought for freedom abroad, but we're not free at home.
<v Zoe Chace>他在东阿卡迪亚的市政厅。他是市议员。他是消防队长。霍勒斯是一名退伍军人、伞兵，在第一次海湾战争中获得铜星勋章。他使用那些民权时代的语言，比如，“我在国外为自由而战，但在家却没有自由。”

00:48:43.950 --> 00:48:57.690
<v Horace Munn>I served to make sure that we all get freedom in America. And I come home, and I find out that they say that we're free but in actuality, we're not. They say things are equal, but in actuality, they're not.
<v Horace Munn>我服役是为了确保我们在美国都能享有自由。我回家后，发现他们说我们是自由的，但实际上我们不是。他们说万物平等，但实际上并非如此。

00:48:57.690 --> 00:49:29.670
<v Zoe Chace>Meaning black voters in this area have been subject to extra scrutiny, to harassment, extra rules, all things that keep them from voting. This is how Horace sees it. The PAC exists to get people to the polls and also bring black people together as a voting bloc. And he says, yes, his group was behind the campaign to get people to write in Franklin Graham for soil and water supervisor. And he explains it. Basically, there was an incumbent running unopposed. They did not like him. So Horace decided, let's get my buddy Franklin Graham on the ballot.
<v Zoe Chace>意思是这个地区的黑人选民一直受到额外审查、骚扰、额外规定，所有这些都阻碍他们投票。霍勒斯就是这么看的。PAC的存在是为了把人们带到投票站，并让黑人作为一个投票集团聚集在一起。他说，是的，他的组织确实支持让人们填写“富兰克林·格雷厄姆”为土壤和水资源主管候选人的运动。他解释了原因。基本上，有一位在职候选人无人竞争。他们不喜欢他。所以霍勒斯决定，让我的伙伴富兰克林·格雷厄姆出现在选票上。

00:49:29.670 --> 00:49:36.560
<v Horace Munn>Well, I missed the window. I missed the window to get his name placed on the ballot.
<v Horace Munn>嗯，我错过了窗口期。我错过了让他的名字出现在选票上的窗口期。

00:49:36.560 --> 00:49:42.850
<v Zoe Chace>Does Franklin Graham actually know anything about what it takes to take care of soil and water as a district supervisor?
<v Zoe Chace>富兰克林·格雷厄姆真的知道担任地区主管需要照顾土壤和水资源吗？

00:49:42.850 --> 00:49:43.880
<v Horace Munn>No.
<v Horace Munn>不知道。

00:49:43.880 --> 00:50:12.890
<v Zoe Chace>The soil and water supervisor, as far as I can figure, keeps the water from being contaminated by the soil and the soil from being eroded by the water. In any way, the incumbent didn't have any special expertise either when he got the job. That's what Horace thinks. I asked Horace about why so many of the ballots seem to have Franklin Graham's name written out in the same handwriting. But he can't discuss the Franklin Graham thing. It's under investigation. And while Lula Pearl's vote seems legit, there are about 300 others that might not be.
<v Zoe Chace>据我所知，土壤和水资源主管负责防止水被土壤污染，并防止土壤被水侵蚀。无论如何，那个在职者在得到这份工作时也没有任何特别的专业知识。霍勒斯就是这么想的。我问霍勒斯为什么这么多选票似乎都是用同样的笔迹写着富兰克林·格雷厄姆的名字。但他无法讨论富兰克林·格雷厄姆的事。它正在调查中。虽然卢拉·珀尔的票看起来合法，但大约有300张可能不是。

00:50:12.890 --> 00:50:23.030
<v Zoe Chace>There is an official body that decides whether it's voter fraud or not, the State Board of Elections. And on a Saturday afternoon, they have a special hearing to decide who's right in this case.
<v Zoe Chace>有一个官方机构来决定那是不是选民欺诈，即州选举委员会。在一个周六下午，他们举行了一次特别听证会来决定谁在这个案子里是对的。

00:50:23.030 --> 00:50:27.840
<v Man 2>We are here to conduct two hearings. We assume jurisdiction that--
<v Man 2>我们在这里举行两场听证会。我们假设管辖权——

00:50:27.840 --> 00:50:52.510
<v Zoe Chace>The board is rushing to rule on all these election protests coming in so the state can actually have a governor. The Republican governor still hasn't conceded the race. The election is up in the air. It's not made for TV. It's not even a courtroom. It's a badly lit press conference-style space. Rows of chairs are just dragged in. There are five board members who will decide things. Three Republicans, two Democrats, a big audience, a slew of lawyers at the table.
<v Zoe Chace>委员会正忙着裁决所有这些涌进来的抗议选举的案件，以便该州能真正拥有一位州长。共和党州长仍然没有承认选举结果。选举悬而未决。这不是为了电视节目准备的。这甚至不是法庭。这是一个照明很差的新闻发布会风格的空间。椅子是临时拖进来的。有五位委员将做出决定。三名共和党人，两名民主党人，一大群观众，桌子旁坐着一大批律师。

00:50:52.510 --> 00:50:56.540
<v Man 3>Did you say the vote should be discounted or the ballots should be discounted?
<v Man 3>你是说选票应该折算，还是应该废弃？

00:50:56.540 --> 00:50:58.280
<v Man 4>The entire ballot should be discounted.
<v Man 4>整张选票都应该废弃。

00:50:58.280 --> 00:51:12.880
<v Zoe Chace>The governor's lawyers come off a little sloppy. Because a few hours in, the complaint starts to fall apart in this kind of mortifying way. It happens when the person who actually filed the complaint against the Bladen County Improvement Association PAC takes the stand.
<v Zoe Chace>州长的律师们看起来有点草率。因为几个小时后，投诉开始以这种令人羞辱的方式分崩离析。这是当真正提出针对布雷登县改进协会PAC投诉的人站在证人席上时发生的。

00:51:12.880 --> 00:51:13.980
<v Man 5>How you doing, sir?
<v Man 5>你好吗，先生？

00:51:13.980 --> 00:51:16.010
<v Mccrae Dowless>Doing fine, Mr. Michael.
<v Mccrae Dowless>很好，迈克尔先生。

00:51:16.010 --> 00:51:37.160
<v Zoe Chace>McCrae Dowless. He's actually the guy who won the soil and water supervisor race in Bladen County. And he takes the stand looking like he's not quite ready for this. He doesn't have a suit. He's got a big beard, skinny guy, windbreaker. And once he's up there being questioned by one of the Democrats on the Board of Elections, he seems amazingly unfamiliar with his own case.
<v Zoe Chace>麦克雷·道利斯。他实际上就是布雷登县土壤和水资源主管选举的获胜者。他站在证人席上，看起来还没准备好。他没穿西装。他留着大胡子，瘦瘦的家伙，穿着防风夹克。当他被选举委员会的一名民主党人审问时，他对自己的案子似乎显得非常陌生。

00:51:37.160 --> 00:51:48.970
<v Man 5>You allege that there was a scheme that was taking place in Bladen County, correct? That's the words that you used-- "blatant scheme"? What did you mean by that?
<v Man 5>你指控布雷登县发生了一个阴谋，对吗？这就是你用的词——“明目张胆的阴谋”？你是什么意思？

00:51:48.970 --> 00:51:50.270
<v Mccrae Dowless>Scheme?
<v Mccrae Dowless>阴谋？

00:51:50.270 --> 00:51:53.970
<v Man 5>You used the words, "resulting from a blatant scheme."
<v Man 5>你用了“源于一个明目张胆的阴谋”这些词。

00:51:53.970 --> 00:51:59.750
<v Mccrae Dowless>Are you saying I used the words or the attorney that wrote that up used the words?
<v Mccrae Dowless>你是说我用了这些词，还是写下这些词的律师用了这些词？

00:51:59.750 --> 00:52:01.130
<v Man 5>Well, it's got your signature at the end.
<v Man 5>嗯，最后有你的签名。

00:52:01.130 --> 00:52:16.580
<v Mccrae Dowless>It's got my signature here on it. But as far as writing that personal-- writing that up, I didn't do that. The attorney did. The attorney was the one that drew the protest up.
<v Mccrae Dowless>这上面确实有我的签名。但就写那个人而言——写下来，我没做。是律师做的。是律师起草了抗议书。

00:52:16.580 --> 00:52:21.350
<v Zoe Chace>He tries to remember who that attorney is, like what his name is.
<v Zoe Chace>他试图回忆那个律师是谁，叫什么名字。

00:52:21.350 --> 00:52:27.710
<v Mccrae Dowless>I can't think of the gentlemen's name. Steve? I can't remember his last name.
<v Mccrae Dowless>我想不起那位先生的名字了。史蒂夫？我不记得他的姓了。

00:52:27.710 --> 00:52:53.460
<v Zoe Chace>His attorneys seem embarrassed. It is embarrassing when a client who's supposedly lodging a complaint is not familiar with the complaint. They try to object. It doesn't work. McCrae just ignores his lawyers and keeps talking over them. I look over at Horace Munn from the PAC, and he is all giddy. He's tapping his lawyer, spinning around in his seat. He pulls his glasses down his nose and makes eyes at me.
<v Zoe Chace>他的律师看起来很尴尬。当一个假定提出投诉的客户不熟悉投诉内容时，这是令人尴尬的。他们试图反对。没用。麦克雷只是无视他的律师，继续说话压过他们。我看向PAC的霍勒斯·曼恩，他兴奋极了。他在拍他的律师，在座位上转来转去。他把眼镜拉下鼻梁，向我抛媚眼。

00:52:53.460 --> 00:53:17.620
<v Zoe Chace>The hearing goes on for five hours. Some people who wrote in the name Franklin Graham on 300-odd ballots broke the rules by not signing the envelope, saying they assisted the voters in filling out the ballots. But over those hours, no one disputes that real voters cast those votes. Finally, one of the Republicans on the election's board says he's not buying this as a massive ballot scheme that's thwarting the Republican governor.
<v Zoe Chace>听证会进行了五个小时。一些人在300多张选票上填写富兰克林·格雷厄姆名字的人打破了规则，没有在信封上签名，说明他们协助选民填写选票。但在这几个小时里，没有人质疑是真实的选民投了这些票。最终，选举委员会的一名共和党人说，他不相信这是一个阻挠共和党州长的巨大选票阴谋。

00:53:17.620 --> 00:53:29.200
<v Man 6>There does not appear to be any dispute that the signatures of the voters on all the ballots are proper. And I think that's something that I would have to hear before all of you wanting to throw out a lot of ballots.
<v Man 6>似乎没有争议认为所有选票上的选民签名是合法的。我想在你们所有人想废弃大量选票之前，我必须先听听那个。

00:53:29.200 --> 00:54:11.590
<v Zoe Chace>The complaint is dismissed, 3 to 2. Dreams of the perfect Republican Christmas gift melt away. I can't say for sure, but in talking to reporters, election board members, activists, it seems that no one in North Carolina was able to find a single case of true voter fraud, where someone deliberately impersonates someone else, casts a ballot in their name, real fraud, except possibly one case. One. And that one comes out in a very strange twist during this same hearing, just not where the Republicans were looking. One of the board members starts asking McCrae, their incumbent Republican soil and water supervisor, about whether his side had committed voter fraud.
<v Zoe Chace>投诉被以3比2驳回。完美的共和党圣诞礼物的梦想融化了。我不敢肯定，但在与记者、选举委员会成员、活动人士交谈后，似乎北卡罗来纳州没有人能找到哪怕一个真正的选民欺诈案例，即某人故意冒充他人，以他们的名义投票，真正的欺诈，除了可能的一起案件。一起。而那一起在同一场听证会上以非常奇怪的转折曝光了，只是不是共和党人寻找的地方。委员会的一名成员开始询问麦克雷，他们现任的共和党土壤和水资源主管，他的这一方是否犯了选民欺诈。

00:54:11.590 --> 00:54:13.580
<v Man 7>So you keep saying GOTV, does that mean--
<v Man 7>所以你一直说GOTV，那是什么意思——

00:54:13.580 --> 00:54:14.570
<v Man 5>Get out the vote. Get out the vote.
<v Man 5>“动员投票”。动员投票。

00:54:14.570 --> 00:54:15.220
<v Man 7>You get paper.
<v Man 7>你拿到纸。

00:54:15.220 --> 00:54:16.500
<v Mccrae Dowless>Get out the vote.
<v Mccrae Dowless>动员投票。

00:54:16.500 --> 00:54:21.220
<v Man 7>And what exactly was it that she got paid to do?
<v Man 7>她到底拿钱做了什么？

00:54:21.220 --> 00:55:08.260
<v Zoe Chace>Here's what tumbles out of McCrae under the board's questioning. He had some people working for him, getting out the vote-- volunteers, McCrae calls them. The volunteers, though, were allegedly getting paid for each ballot they turned in. That is illegal. One of the voters who signed an affidavit said that Get Out the Vote workers came by and had her family request absentee ballots. But then they never received their absentee ballots in the mail like they were supposed to. Then, when the family went to vote on election day, they were told they'd already voted. In essence, McCrae's getting accused of paying people to obtain absentee ballots, fill them out, and cast their votes on someone else's behalf. That, for sure, is illegal. McCrae says he didn't do anything wrong.
<v Zoe Chace>这就是麦克雷在委员会盘问下吐露的内容。他有一些人为他工作，动员投票——麦克雷称他们为志愿者。然而，这些志愿者据称是每交出一张选票就能得到报酬。这是违法的。一名签署宣誓书的选民说，“动员投票”的工作人员来过，并让她家人申请了缺席选票。但后来他们从未像预期的那样在邮寄中收到缺席选票。然后，当这家人在选举日去投票时，他们被告知他们已经投过票了。本质上，麦克雷被指控花钱雇人获取缺席选票、填写并代表他人投票。那，绝对是违法的。麦克雷说他没做错什么。

00:55:08.260 --> 00:55:14.540
<v Zoe Chace>An election board member then calls for further criminal investigation, what appears to be Republican voter fraud.
<v Zoe Chace>一名选举委员会成员随后呼吁进行进一步的刑事调查，这看起来像是共和党选民欺诈。

00:55:14.540 --> 00:55:24.880
<v Man 8>I will be making a motion, that any and all information that this board has in its possession shall be forwarded to the United States Attorney for the Eastern District of North Carolina.
<v Man 8>我将动议，本委员会拥有的所有信息应转发给美国北卡罗来纳州东区检察官。

00:55:24.880 --> 00:55:49.860
<v Zoe Chace>The attorney could choose to keep looking into Horace as well, but the focus of the investigation seems to be what McCrae has just revealed. Two days later, the governor of North Carolina concedes the race to the Democrats. There are just no votes left to recount, no more fraud left to look into.
<v Zoe Chace>检察官也可以选择继续调查霍勒斯，但调查的焦点似乎是麦克雷刚刚揭露的内容。两天后，北卡罗来纳州州长承认输给了民主党人。没有选票可以重新计票，没有更多的欺诈可以调查。

00:55:49.860 --> 00:56:15.390
<v Zoe Chace>Dallas Woodhouse, the Republican fraud hunter, says there is still systemic voter fraud out there, stuff that's not being caught. And that is going to continue until the voting system itself changes. Right now, Dallas would say there are too many opportunities for voter fraud, too many ways that people could pretend to be someone else. Right now, you show up to vote with no ID, they let you vote. You show up at the wrong precinct, they let you vote.
<v Zoe Chace>共和党欺诈调查员达拉斯·伍德豪斯说，外面仍然存在系统性的选民欺诈，那些还没被抓住的东西。在投票系统本身改变之前，这种情况将会持续。现在，达拉斯会说，有太多的机会进行选民欺诈，太多人可以假装成别人。现在，你没有任何证件去投票，他们让你投。你在错误的选区出现，他们让你投。

00:56:15.390 --> 00:56:25.780
<v Dallas Woodhouse>Our side does think the voters have some responsibilities. And the best example is showing up to the right place to vote on election day. I do think that's a fair statement.
<v Dallas Woodhouse>我们这一方确实认为选民有一些责任。最好的例子就是在选举日去正确的地点投票。我认为这是一个公平的陈述。

00:56:25.780 --> 00:56:31.560
<v Zoe Chace>I think that is the exact thing that Democrats will point to saying, that disenfranchises our people because our people--
<v Zoe Chace>我认为这正是民主党人会指出的，称那剥夺了我们的人民，因为我们的人民——

00:56:31.560 --> 00:56:32.640
<v Dallas Woodhouse>How does it disenfranchise their people?
<v Dallas Woodhouse>它怎么剥夺了他们的人民？

00:56:32.640 --> 00:56:33.960
<v Zoe Chace>Because their people--
<v Zoe Chace>因为他们的人民——

00:56:33.960 --> 00:56:45.900
<v Dallas Woodhouse>See, they have such a negative, horrible, demeaning view of their own people, that they can't show up at the right place to vote.
<v Dallas Woodhouse>瞧，他们对他们自己的人民有一种如此消极、可怕、贬低的态度，以至于他们不能去正确的地点投票。

00:56:45.900 --> 00:57:18.000
<v Zoe Chace>The two parties have very different ideas about how we should vote. To generalize, Democrats think it should be as easy as possible. Republicans don't. Conveniently, each of these positions ends up helping their side at the polls. Tougher voter requirements tend to disproportionately discourage minority voters. That traditionally helps Republicans. Higher voter turnout of minority voters usually helps the Democrats. Dallas, a passionate partisan, will keep arguing and keep looking for fraud, though he keeps losing these fights.
<v Zoe Chace>两党对我们应该如何投票有截然不同的想法。概括地说，民主党人认为应该尽可能简单。共和党人不这么认为。方便的是，这些立场中的每一个最终都帮助了他们这一方在投票站获胜。更严格的投票要求往往会不成比例地阻碍少数族裔选民。这传统上帮助共和党人。少数族裔选民的更高投票率通常帮助民主党人。达拉斯，一个热情的党派人士，将继续争论并继续寻找欺诈，尽管他一直在输掉这些战斗。

00:57:18.000 --> 00:57:36.990
<v Zoe Chace>The North Carolina State Board of Elections tracked 57 voter fraud complaints this year and dismissed the vast majority of them. For those searching for voter fraud, it's like they waited all election year, opened up their presents Christmas morning, and there was nothing inside.
<v Zoe Chace>北卡罗来纳州选举委员会今年追踪了57起选民欺诈投诉，驳回了其中的绝大多数。对于那些寻找选民欺诈的人来说，就像他们等了整个选举年，圣诞早晨打开礼物，里面却空空如也。

00:57:36.990 --> 00:57:40.210
<v Ira Glass>Zoe Chace is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>佐伊·蔡斯是我们节目的制作人之一。

